            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Explain the two characteristic features of carbon that make it form a large number of compounds. How do these features give rise to a large variety of organic compounds?"
            }
          ]
        },
        "topic_names": [
          "Versatile Nature of Carbon"
        ],
        "answer": "The two features are catenation and tetravalency. Catenation is the ability of carbon to form bonds with other carbon atoms, creating long chains, branched chains, and rings. Tetravalency means carbon can form four covalent bonds, allowing it to bond with hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, etc. Together, these features allow carbon to form millions of stable compounds with different structures and functional groups.",
